-- title:
--   Fourier partial sums are best approximations
-- statement:
--   Let $e_1, \dots, e_n$ be an **orthonormal** family in a real inner product space $H$, and let $x \in H$. Then the best approximation to $x$ from the subspace generated by $e_1, \dots, e_n$ is the **Fourier partial sum**
--
--   $$\hat x = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \langle x,\, e_i\rangle\, e_i,$$
--
--   whose coefficients $\langle x, e_i\rangle$ are the **Fourier coefficients** of $x$ with respect to the family. That is, $\|x - \hat x\| \le \|x - m\|$ for every $m$ in that subspace.
--
--   This is the general approximation problem in the case where the Gram matrix of the generating vectors is the identity: the normal equations decouple, and each coefficient is read off directly instead of being solved for. It is also why the Gram–Schmidt procedure is useful in practice — once a generating family has been orthonormalized, the best approximation to *any* vector is immediate.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The subspace is the algebraic span of the family, not its closure; the claim is the family of norm inequalities, with no uniqueness assertion attached.
-- source:
--   David G. Luenberger, Optimization by Vector Space Methods, John Wiley & Sons, 1969, §3.7 p. 58 and §3.9 p. 63

import Mathlib
open scoped RealInnerProductSpace

namespace VectorSpaceOpt

theorem fourier_best_approximation {H : Type} [NormedAddCommGroup H]
    [InnerProductSpace ℝ H] {n : ℕ} (e : Fin n → H) (he : Orthonormal ℝ e) (x : H) :
    ∀ m ∈ Submodule.span ℝ (Set.range e),
      ‖x - ∑ i, ⟪x, e i⟫ • e i‖ ≤ ‖x - m‖ := by sorry

end VectorSpaceOpt
